# Every text file in this repository is LF, in the index and in the working
# tree.
#
# `text=auto` on its own normalises what Git stores but leaves the checkout to
# the platform, so a Windows working tree gets CRLF. Pinning `eol` per extension
# fixed that for the three that were listed and silently missed everything else:
# `scripts/*.mjs` were CRLF while `src/*.js` were LF, `LICENSE` and this file
# were CRLF, and `.gitignore` had ended up with mixed endings. That inconsistency
# is a real hazard rather than a cosmetic one — a patch script written for one
# convention corrupts a file written in the other.
#
# Setting `eol` on the default rule covers every text file, including ones with
# extensions nobody has thought of yet, which is why there are no per-extension
# rules here any more.
* text=auto eol=lf
